<div class="cosmetics-injection">
  <section class="cosmetics-faq-block" aria-labelledby="cosmetics-faq-heading">
    <h2 id="cosmetics-faq-heading">
      Cosmetic Manufacturing Machinery FAQ – Sri Lanka
    </h2>
    <p class="faq-lead">
      Practical answers for Sri Lankan cosmetic, skincare, haircare, herbal, and personal care manufacturers looking for reliable filling, sealing, coding, and packaging machinery.
    </p>

    <div class="faq-list">
      <details class="faq-item" open>
        <summary>What filling machines do Sri Lankan cosmetic manufacturers use for shampoo, liquid hand wash, and body lotion?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            For free-flowing liquids like hair oil, toners, and serums, manufacturers commonly use digital or CNC liquid filling machines, while thicker formulations like <strong>shampoo, liquid soap, shower gel, body lotion, and face cream</strong> benefit from volumetric <strong>piston or pneumatic paste/cream filling machines</strong> with anti-drip nozzles and stainless steel contact parts.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Can one machine fill both thin cosmetic liquids and thick creams or gels?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            An <strong>automatic in-line piston liquid and paste filling machine</strong> can be configured across multiple volume ranges (such as 10ml–100ml up to larger containers) to handle varying viscosities—from watery liquids to thicker creams, gels, and spreads—while keeping volume variation tight and minimizing costly formulation waste.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How do cosmetic brands print batch numbers and expiry dates on plastic, glass, and metal containers?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Cosmetic producers in Sri Lanka typically pair their filling line with a <strong>date coding / batch printing machine</strong>—using quick-dry solvent inkjet printers for smooth plastic/glass bottles and jars, or thermal transfer / hot-foil / ribbon coders for labels and pouches—so batch codes and expiry dates stay legible despite moisture or oil handling.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What secondary packaging machines complete a cosmetic bottle or jar line?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Beyond filling, cosmetic lines often use <strong>capping machines</strong>, <strong>induction sealers</strong> (for tamper evidence and leak protection), <strong>shrink tunnel machines</strong> (for neck bands or full-body sleeves), and <strong>labelling machines</strong>—plus conveyors to keep bottle transfer steady.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What should I check before buying cosmetic packaging machinery in Sri Lanka?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Check your <strong>container neck/opening size and height</strong>, <strong>product viscosity and foaming/separation behavior</strong>, <strong>target bottles per minute</strong>, <strong>power and air compressor needs</strong>, and <strong>local spare parts and after-sales technician availability</strong> in Sri Lanka before ordering.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

